.SS "enum \fBSystemEx\&.Endian\fP"

.PP
Specifies the byte order used when converting values to and from raw byte sequences\&. Endianness determines how multi‑byte numeric values are represented in memory and serialized into byte arrays\&. All conversion methods in SystemEx\&.Conversion support explicit endianness to ensure deterministic binary layouts across platforms\&. 

.PP
The enumeration provides three modes: 
.PD 0
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBEndian\&.LittleEndian\fP — least significant byte first\&.   
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBEndian\&.BigEndian\fP — most significant byte first\&.   
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBEndian\&.System\fP — use the native endianness of the current machine as detected by SystemEx\&.Conversion\&.GetEndian\&.   
.PP

.PP
\fBNote:\fP When \fBEndian\&.System\fP is specified, conversion methods do not perform byte‑swapping\&. They serialize values exactly as the CPU represents them in memory\&. This is typically little‑endian on modern x86/x64 systems\&. 
.PP
\fBEnumerator\fP
.in +1c
.TP
\f(BILittleEndian \fP
Least significant byte first (little‑endian)\&. This is the native format on most modern architectures including x86 and x64\&. 
.TP
\f(BIBigEndian \fP
Most significant byte first (big‑endian)\&. Common in network protocols and some older CPU architectures\&. 
.TP
\f(BISystem \fP
Uses the system’s native endianness as detected by SystemEx\&.Conversion\&.GetEndian\&. This mode ensures that values are serialized exactly as the CPU stores them in memory, without performing any byte‑swapping\&. 
.PP
